From c10a2041915a02b2bb8975e31e32cfff3a91e911 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Josh Lukens <jlukens@botch.com>
Date: Sun, 15 Nov 2026 14:37:38 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] Add optional RF listener support

A PLM hears only what reaches its own antenna. Battery devices (leak,
door, motion, remotes) are RF-only and can't be polled, so a message the
modem misses from one of them is lost. Receive-only RF listeners placed
elsewhere in the house (an ESPHome board with a CC1101, an RTL-SDR, an
rfcat dongle) decode what they hear and publish it to MQTT. This compares
what they heard with what the modem heard and hands the modem's misses to
the protocol stack. Nothing is transmitted; the modem stays the only
transmitter.

New optional `mqtt.listeners` config section. Without it nothing is
created and nothing changes.

Listener messages (default topic insteon-rf/msg/+), one per transmission:

    {"v": 1, "rx": "t-embed", "t": <on-air epoch s>,
     "frame": "0250...", "copies": 3, "hops_left": 2,
     "rssi": -78.5, "snr": null, "repaired": 0}

'frame' is the message exactly as the modem would report it (0x50/0x51).

How a message is decided (Listeners.py):

- Copies of the same bytes from any listeners within 0.5 s on air are one
  transmission; further apart they are separate ones. Messages with a
  non-finite 't', or one more than 10 s from now, are dropped.
- Each is held 2.5 s after it arrives for the modem's own copy, which
  often comes later over the powerline. Decisions use insteon-mqtt's own
  clock; a listener's clock only orders transmissions.
- Nothing more happens if the modem read it (each modem read is credited
  to one event; a read of the same bytes within 2 s on air also counts,
  covering device retransmissions), if it read the other form of the same
  event (broadcast vs cleanup, within 8 s), or if it has since read a
  newer message about the same state from that device (leak sensors' dry
  and wet groups count as one state). Messages to another device, and
  group broadcasts the modem holds no link for, are not modem misses.
- The rest are classified: 'battery' (broadcasts and cleanups from
  battery devices), 'group' (other group broadcasts and cleanups), 'state'
  (other messages to the modem). Never handled: ACK/NAK, cleanup status
  reports (0x06), the modem's own transmissions, unknown senders, messages
  a listener repaired, and group 0 broadcasts, which publish an alert.
- Tiers listed in 'inject' go to the new Protocol.inject(); tiers in
  'shadow' are only logged ("Would inject ..."). Rate limits: 4 per device
  and 12 overall per minute. Events are released oldest first; an error
  deciding one does not affect the others.
- Counters per listener and per device are published retained to a stats
  topic; group 0 broadcasts go to an alert topic.

Protocol gains signal_raw_read (every frame read from the modem) and
inject(), which accepts only complete 0x50/0x51 frames of the exact length
and passes them through the normal duplicate check and handlers.

